{"agent":"fx-phase-deps","data":{"by":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Pro-0813-Godzila","evidence":{"chunk_findings":[{"chunk":1,"detail":"Previous finding 1 still holds. With `[gates].doc_globs = [\"docs/**\"]` and a docs-only task whose `globs = [\"docs/**/*.md\"]`, `_glob_is_docs` returns `False` because `\"**\" in glob` and the task glob is not literally in `doc_globs`. Every path matched by `docs/**/*.md` is under `docs/**`, so all task globs are documents under the documented contract, yet the task still inherits the phase `needs` and can remain blocked. The current probe logic does not address this branch.","digest":"cf8036ebec46fac2","location":"ddflow/core/schedule.py:_glob_is_docs","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/core/schedule.py:_glob_is_docs"},{"chunk":1,"detail":"Previous finding 2 still holds in substance despite the added second probe. With `[gates].doc_globs = [\"docs/x\", \"docs/zq9\"]` and a task glob `docs/*`, `_glob_is_docs` produces the probes `docs/x` and `docs/zq9`; both are doc globs, so it returns `True`. But `docs/*` also matches `docs/y`, which is not in `doc_globs`, so a task that can match/write non-doc files is incorrectly exempted from `phase.needs`. Finite probe strings are not a sound inclusion test for glob languages.","digest":"355834c1fba59ceb","location":"ddflow/core/schedule.py:_glob_is_docs","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/core/schedule.py:_glob_is_docs"},{"chunk":2,"detail":"The previous probe still holds. With `[gates].doc_globs = [\"docs/**\"]` and a docs-only task whose `globs = [\"docs/**/*.md\"]`, `_glob_is_docs` returns `False` because the task glob contains `**` and is not literally present in `doc_globs`. Every path matching `docs/**/*.md` is under the configured doc glob, so the task is docs-only and should be exempt from `phase.needs`, but it still inherits them and remains blocked. The reviewed part does not alter this branch, so the defect is still present.","digest":"2eea26e4209c7495","location":"ddflow/core/schedule.py:_glob_is_docs","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/core/schedule.py:_glob_is_docs"},{"chunk":2,"detail":"The previous `*`/`?` probe is still not a sound inclusion test. With `[gates].doc_globs = [\"docs/x*\"]` and a task glob `docs/*`, the probe checks `docs/x`; `path_in_glob(\"docs/x\", \"docs/x*\")` is true, so `_docs_only_exempt` skips phase dependencies. But `docs/*` also matches `docs/y`, which is not in `doc_globs`; a task that can write non-doc files is therefore incorrectly exempted from `phase.needs`. The reviewed part leaves this probe unchanged.","digest":"ad8dae625f591436","location":"ddflow/core/schedule.py:_glob_is_docs","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/core/schedule.py:_glob_is_docs"},{"chunk":3,"detail":"The earlier finding still holds. The new tests do not cover the failing input `[gates].doc_globs = [\"docs/**\"]` with a docs-only task whose `globs = [\"docs/**/*.md\"]`. `test_docs_only_tasks_are_ready_code_tasks_still_wait` uses only `docs/n.md` and `docs/ddflow/research/n.md`, and `test_a_recursive_glob_is_not_docs_under_a_narrower_doc_glob` exercises the opposite containment (`docs/**` task glob under narrower `docs/*`). With the branch `if \"**\" in glob: return False`, `_glob_is_docs(\"docs/**/*.md\", [\"docs/**\"])` still returns `False`, so such a docs-only task still inherits `phase.needs` and remains blocked even though every path it matches is under `docs/**`.","digest":"ca1adbca40e42fc4","location":"ddflow/core/schedule.py:_glob_is_docs","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/core/schedule.py:_glob_is_docs"}],"chunks_total":3,"coverage":"3/3 chunk(s) reviewed","delta_rounds":0,"diff_chars":11075,"diff_sha":"8c5ee8cb5981eaf1eba52e93ad9bfd8faf036d9fda84c39f975ef265e833aea9","diff_source":"main..HEAD + tracked working-tree changes in [REDACTED:path]","diff_stat":{"deletions":0,"files":0,"insertions":0,"untracked":0},"elapsed_s":287.7,"excluded_bytes":2687,"excluded_files":1,"excluded_paths":["README.md"],"family":"deepseek","finding